{"id":"containers-images","corpus":"k8s","title":"Images","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/images/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Images"},{"kind":"p","text":"A container image represents binary data that encapsulates an application and all its software dependencies. Container images are executable software bundles that can run standalone and that make very well-defined assumptions about their runtime environment."},{"kind":"p","text":"You typically create a container image of your application and push it to a registry before referring to it in a Pod."},{"kind":"p","text":"This page provides an outline of the container image concept."},{"kind":"p","text":"If you are looking for the container images for a Kubernetes release (such as v1.37, the latest minor release), visit Download Kubernetes."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Image names"},{"kind":"p","text":"Container images are usually given a name such as pause, example/mycontainer, or kube-apiserver. Images can also include a registry hostname; for example: fictional.registry.example/imagename, and possibly a port number as well; for example: fictional.registry.example:10443/imagename."},{"kind":"p","text":"If you don't specify a registry hostname, Kubernetes assumes that you mean the Docker public registry. You can change this behavior by setting a default image registry in the container runtime configuration."},{"kind":"p","text":"After the image name part you can add a tag or digest (in the same way you would when using with commands like docker or podman). Tags let you identify different versions of the same series of images. Digests are a unique identifier for a specific version of an image. Digests are hashes of the image's content, and are immutable. Tags can be moved to point to different images, but digests are fixed."},{"kind":"p","text":"Image tags consist of lowercase and uppercase letters, digits, underscores (_), periods (.), and dashes (-). A tag can be up to 128 characters long, and must conform to the following regex pattern: [a-zA-Z0-9_][a-zA-Z0-9._-]{0,127}. You can read more about it and find the validation regex in the OCI Distribution Specification. If you don't specify a tag, Kubernetes assumes you mean the tag latest."},{"kind":"p","text":"Image digests consists of a hash algorithm (such as sha256) and a hash value. For example: sha256:1ff6c18fbef2045af6b9c16bf034cc421a29027b800e4f9b68ae9b1cb3e9ae07. You can find more information about the digest format in the OCI Image Specification."},{"kind":"p","text":"Some image name examples that Kubernetes can use are:"},{"kind":"li","text":"busybox — Image name only, no tag or digest. Kubernetes will use the Docker public registry and latest tag. Equivalent to docker.io/library/busybox:latest."},{"kind":"li","text":"busybox:1.32.0 — Image name with tag. Kubernetes will use the Docker public registry. Equivalent to docker.io/library/busybox:1.32.0."},{"kind":"li","text":"registry.k8s.io/pause:latest — Image name with a custom registry and latest tag."},{"kind":"li","text":"registry.k8s.io/pause:3.5 — Image name with a custom registry and non-latest tag."},{"kind":"li","text":"registry.k8s.io/pause@sha256:1ff6c18fbef2045af6b9c16bf034cc421a29027b800e4f9b68ae9b1cb3e9ae07 — Image name with digest."},{"kind":"li","text":"registry.k8s.io/pause:3.5@sha256:1ff6c18fbef2045af6b9c16bf034cc421a29027b800e4f9b68ae9b1cb3e9ae07 — Image name with tag and digest. Only the digest will be used for pulling."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Updating images"},{"kind":"p","text":"When you first create a Deployment, StatefulSet, Pod, or other object that includes a PodTemplate, and a pull policy was not explicitly specified, then by default the pull policy of all containers in that Pod will be set to IfNotPresent. This policy causes the kubelet to skip pulling an image if it already exists."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Image pull policy"},{"kind":"p","text":"The imagePullPolicy for a container and the tag of the image both affect when the kubelet attempts to pull (download) the specified image."},{"kind":"p","text":"Here's a list of the values you can set for imagePullPolicy and the effects these values have:"},{"kind":"p","text":"IfNotPresent"},{"kind":"p","text":"the image is pulled only if it is not already present locally."},{"kind":"p","text":"Always"},{"kind":"p","text":"every time the kubelet launches a container, the kubelet requests the container runtime to pull the image. The container runtime contacts the registry, resolves the image tag or name to a digest, and downloads any layers that are not already cached locally. If all layers are already present, the container runtime uses the cached image without downloading it again. The kubelet itself does not check whether the image is cached locally; it always delegates to the container runtime."},{"kind":"p","text":"Never"},{"kind":"p","text":"the kubelet does not try fetching the image. If the image is somehow already present locally, the kubelet attempts to start the container; otherwise, startup fails. See pre-pulled images for more details."},{"kind":"p","text":"The caching semantics of the container runtime make even imagePullPolicy: Always efficient, as long as the registry is reliably accessible. The container runtime can notice that the image layers already exist on the node so that they don't need to be downloaded again."}],"lemmas":{"images":["image","images"],"imaged":["imaged","image"],"imaging":["imaging","image"],"represents":["represents","represent"],"represented":["represented","represent"],"representing":["representing","represent"],"binaries":["binary","binaries"],"data":["datum","data"],"encapsulates":["encapsulates","encapsulate"],"applications":["application","applications"],"its":["its","it"],"dependencies":["dependency","dependencies"],"executables":["executables","executabl